<!-- SECTION 1: EXECUTIVE SUMMARY -->
<h1>1. Executive Summary</h1>
<p>
    Inventory mismanagement represents one of the largest drains on profitability in retail enterprises. Traditional static replenishment heuristics (e.g., ordering static batches on fixed calendar days) frequently lead to either <strong>stockouts</strong> (lost revenue, dissatisfied customers) or <strong>overstocking</strong> (dead capital, inflated warehousing costs, product obsolescence).
</p>
<p>
    This project designs and implements an end-to-end, production-grade <strong>Optimised Retail Inventory Management System</strong>. The system models 2 years of daily sales transactions across 20 retail products and 3 distinct categories (Electronics, Groceries, Apparel). Using machine learning (Random Forest Regressor) with lag features and calendar seasonality, the system forecasts future daily demand with a <strong>54.6% reduction in Mean Absolute Error (MAE)</strong> compared to classical moving-average baselines. Forecasted demand is dynamically integrated into classical inventory control formulas—<strong>Safety Stock (SS)</strong>, <strong>Reorder Point (ROP)</strong>, and <strong>Economic Order Quantity (EOQ)</strong>—and presented through an interactive, multi-tab Streamlit executive dashboard.
</p>

<!-- SECTION 2: PROBLEM STATEMENT & OBJECTIVES -->
<h1>2. Problem Statement & Objectives</h1>
<p>
    Retailers face two opposing operational risks:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Understocking (Stockouts):</strong> When demand surges, shelves run empty, causing immediate revenue loss and customer attrition.</li>
    <li><strong>Overstocking:</strong> Tying up operating cash flow in inventory, leading to high holding costs (rent, insurance, security, refrigeration) and risks of spoilage.</li>
</ul>
<p>
    <strong>Project Objectives:</strong>
</p>
<ol>
    <li>Generate a statistically sound synthetic retail transaction dataset representing 20 SKUs over 730 days (14,600 rows) with realistic seasonality and promotional behavior.</li>
    <li>Train an autoregressive machine learning model using leakage-free temporal, lag, and rolling features evaluated on an unseen 60-day test set.</li>
    <li>Compute exact Safety Stock ($SS$), Reorder Point ($ROP$), and Economic Order Quantity ($EOQ$) per SKU.</li>
    <li>Provide an intuitive, responsive Streamlit dashboard with interactive Plotly Pareto curves, SKU-level historical vs predicted trendlines, and a color-coded actionable purchase order table with CSV export.</li>
</ol>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- SECTION 3: SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE & FEATURE ENGINEERING -->
<h1>3. System Architecture & Methodology</h1>
<p>
    The system architecture separates data synthesis, ML model training/inference, mathematical inventory formulation, and UI visualization:
</p>
<div class="formula">
[data_generator.py]  --> Generates 2 Years of Retail Sales Transactions (14,600 rows)<br>
        |<br>
        v<br>
[model.py]           --> Feature Engineering + Random Forest Regressor (MAE 5.03)<br>
        |<br>
        v<br>
[inventory_logic.py] --> Computes Safety Stock, Reorder Point, EOQ, ABC Tiers<br>
        |<br>
        v<br>
[app.py]             --> Streamlit Executive Cockpit (Overview, Forecasts, Orders)
</div>

<h2>3.1 Feature Engineering Pipeline</h2>
<table>
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Feature Name</th>
            <th>Type</th>
            <th>Mathematical Formulation / Logic</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>day_of_week</code></td><td>Temporal</td><td>0 (Monday) to 6 (Sunday) – Captures weekend consumer surges.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>month</code></td><td>Temporal</td><td>1 to 12 – Captures annual festival surges (Q4 holiday surge).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>price</code></td><td>Financial</td><td>Discounted unit selling price during promotional campaigns.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>promotion</code></td><td>Binary Indicator</td><td>1 if discount campaign active (10–20% discount), 0 otherwise.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>lag_7</code></td><td>Autoregressive</td><td>Sales volume 7 days prior: <code>shift(7)</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>lag_14</code></td><td>Autoregressive</td><td>Sales volume 14 days prior: <code>shift(14)</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>rolling_mean_7</code></td><td>Trend</td><td>7-day moving average: <code>shift(1).rolling(7).mean()</code> (Zero Leakage).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>rolling_mean_30</code></td><td>Baseline</td><td>30-day baseline consumption: <code>shift(1).rolling(30).mean()</code>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<!-- SECTION 4: INVENTORY OPTIMIZATION FORMULAS -->
<h1>4. Inventory Optimization Mathematics</h1>

<h2>4.1 Safety Stock (SS)</h2>
<div class="formula">
    SS = Z * sqrt(Lead Time) * sigma_d<br>
    Z = 1.65 (corresponds to a 95% cycle service level)<br>
    Lead Time = Supplier fulfillment lead time in days<br>
    sigma_d = Standard deviation of daily demand
</div>

<h2>4.2 Reorder Point (ROP)</h2>
<div class="formula">
    ROP = (Average Daily Demand * Lead Time) + Safety Stock<br>
    Triggers purchase order placement when current warehouse stock drops to or below ROP.
</div>

<h2>4.3 Economic Order Quantity (EOQ)</h2>
<div class="formula">
    EOQ = sqrt( (2 * Annual Demand * Ordering Cost) / Holding Cost )<br>
    Balances fixed order placement expenses against annual warehouse inventory holding costs.
</div>

<h2>4.4 ABC Pareto Classification</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Class A:</strong> Top ~70% of total annual revenue. High priority; requires tight daily control.</li>
    <li><strong>Class B:</strong> Next ~20% of revenue. Moderate priority.</li>
    <li><strong>Class C:</strong> Bottom ~10% of revenue. Bulk-ordered with basic periodic reviews.</li>
</ul>

